::: {.doc-subject}

Check if a set d-separates two others and no proper subset does.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L67145-L67256){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.is_minimal_d_separator(
    x,
    y,
    z,
    included=None,
    restricted=None,
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Is minimal d-separator](../assets/graphs/is-minimal-d-separator.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="The chain A to B to C to D, with A in teal and D in violet. Left: z = [B] (outlined) blocks the chain, and dropping B would open it, so is_minimal_d_separator returns True. Right: z = [B, C] also separates A from D, but C (accent) is not needed, since B alone already blocks the chain, so the set is not minimal and the call returns False." width="100%"}

A d-separator `z` of `x` and `y` (see `is_d_separator()`) is *minimal* when removing any
node from it breaks the separation. Minimal separators are the smallest sets of variables
that must be measured to make `x` and `y` conditionally independent, which keeps causal
adjustment sets lean. With `included=`, nodes that must be in the separator are fixed and
only the other nodes have to be needed; with `restricted=`, the separator may only use
the given nodes.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[x]{.doc-parameter-name}</code>

:   Nodes, or lists of nodes, as IDs or labels.

<code>[y]{.doc-parameter-name}</code>

:   Nodes, or lists of nodes, as IDs or labels.

<code>[z]{.doc-parameter-name}</code>

:   The candidate separator (a node or a list).

<code>[included]{.doc-parameter-name}[=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op}[None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional nodes that are required to be in `z` (and are exempt from minimality).

<code>[restricted]{.doc-parameter-name}[=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op}[None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional nodes that `z` must be drawn from (default: all nodes).
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   `True` if `z` is a minimal d-separator of `x` and `y` under the constraints.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If the graph is not a directed acyclic graph, a node does not exist, the sets are not
    disjoint, `included` is not part of `z`, or `z` is not part of `restricted`.
:::

## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}

The test of van der Zander, Liśkiewicz and Textor (2019), as in NetworkX: `z` must lie
within the ancestors of `x`, `y` and `included`, must not let `x` reach `y`, and every
node of `z` outside `included` must be reachable (by open paths given `z`) from both `x`
and `y`, so dropping it would open a path. It runs in $O(V + E)$ time.

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~is_d_separator`) 

:   Check a separator, minimal or not.

[](`~find_minimal_d_separator`) 

:   Find a minimal separator.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

`B` alone separates the ends of a chain, so adding `D` to it gives a separator that isn't
minimal:

::: {#78ecc5f8 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [("A", "B"), ("B", "C"), ("D", "C")], directed=True
)
(
    graph.is_minimal_d_separator("A", "C", ["B"]),
    graph.is_minimal_d_separator("A", "C", ["B", "D"]),
)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(True, False)
```
:::
:::


### Nothing to spare

`causal()` is a causal diagram of the risks for heart disease, with an arrow from each
cause to its effect. Blood pressure and Genetics separate Age from Heart disease, and
neither can be left out. Adding Medication still separates them, but no longer
minimally:

::: {#fe2c330a .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
causal = ec.causal()
pair = ("Age", "Heart disease")

(
    causal.is_minimal_d_separator(*pair, ["Blood pressure", "Genetics"]),
    causal.is_minimal_d_separator(
        *pair, ["Blood pressure", "Genetics", "Medication"]
    ),
)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
(True, False)
```
:::
:::


::: {#38c2c48e .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
causal.plot(
    layout="pos",
    highlight={"accent": ["Blood pressure", "Genetics"],
               "soft": ["Age", "Heart disease"]},
    label_by="label", height=360,
)
```

